H1.1->retired, H1.1.2->refuted, H1.3.1->inconclusive, H1.5->inconclusive, H1.6->inconclusive, H1.6.1->refuted, H1.7.1->inconclusive, H1.7.2->refuted, H1.7.3->inconclusive, H1.7.4->retired, H1.7.5->retired, H2.3->supported, H2.4.1->refuted, H2.4.2->supported, H2.5->supported, H2.5.1->supported, H3->supported, H3.2->supported, H3.2.1->supported, H3.2.2->supported, H4.2->inconclusive, H4.2.1->refuted, +H1.8, +H1.8.1, +H1.6.2, +H1.7.6, +H2.3.2, +H2.3.3, +H2.3.4, +H2.4.4, +H2.5.2, +H4.3, +H5, +H5.1. The tree now treats noise as the main problem, matching the review. H4 is rewritten as a decision/selection-noise hypothesis, the salt replicate (H4.1.1) is retargeted onto E021, and a winner's-curse dev-seed prediction is added (N9). Verdicts inside the ~0.2 pp band are downgraded: H1.3.1 and H1.7.3 to inconclusive, H4.2 to inconclusive. H1.1.2 is changed to refuted, because 'supports' rested on one clause of the prediction. The SGD branch H1.1 and H1.7.4 are retired; H1.7.5 is retired because the schedule already decays only to ~0.07x. H1.5 is set to inconclusive: its evidence was misfiled and the E008 record is inconsistent. H1.7 is lowered to 0.3 and rewritten as an untuned Muon lr/momentum hypothesis, since E016 shows the SGD-era parts do useful work. H2.4.1 is refuted by the profiler (23% non-conv time) and H2.4 is rewritten around conv efficiency in the high-resolution stage. Marked supported: H2.3 (resizing, -4.2% time), H2.4.2 and H2.5/H2.5.1 (CUDA graph, -3%). H3 and H3.2.x are supported and closed as exhausted. New nodes: a wide-span epoch-slope measurement (N1/N1a) to replace the unmeasured ~1 pp/s line; a lighter loss filter to settle H1.6; a Muon lr sweep; FixRes/fast-BN-momentum, low-res batch-size and resolution-tied regularization children under H2.3; whitening-bias freezing (N7); NS 3 to 2 plus periodic renormalization (N8); and a new Lvl1 on transfer to the PCIe judging GPU and the 40-seed mean (N10). Suggested order: salt replicates and dev seeds on E021, then the wide-span slope, then NS=2 and whitening-bias freezing, then the resizing-schedule sweep.
added I035, I036, I037, I038, I039, I040; dropped none. The queue had no queued ideas left, so I added 6 (4 exploit, 2 explore). The binding constraint is margin: pooling E025 and its salted re-run (E027) puts the incumbent's expected accuracy at about 0.7533, right at the 0.753 bar. Accuracy deltas are judged against that pooled figure, not E025's single draw of 0.7539.

1. **Muon lr 1.25x (H1.7.6), priority 5.** A free accuracy gain is the most valuable result now. The rationale is that Turbo preconditioning shrank the effective update by about a third.
2. **Whitening bias trained only in the 24 px phase, then detached (H2.4.4), priority 4.** This removes work in the 28/32 px phases and needs no extra graph. Expected time -1 to -2% with accuracy unchanged.
3. **20 px for the first 2 epochs (H2.3), priority 3.** Extends the resolution ladder. Expected -3 to -4% time for about -0.15 pp; pair it with any margin gain.
4. **Batch 3072 in the 24 px phase only (H2.3.3), priority 2.** Halves the fixed Muon cost per low-res step. Riskier for accuracy.
5. **+0.5 epoch at 32 px (H1.8.1), priority 2.** Measures the pp/s slope and gives a safe fallback config. It won't beat E025 on time, so it is calibration only.
6. **Label smoothing 0.4 (H1.3.1), priority 2.** The last untested direction of that knob; time-neutral.

No queued ideas needed dropping. I left H1.2.1 alone because alternating flip is already in the recipe, and skipped H1.6.2 and H2.4.3 because their priors are weak. H4.3 (dev seeds) isn't a file change, so it can't be an idea here.
